le\n    strings of the same length. Return None in case the input list is empty.\n    >>> longest([])\n\n    >>> longest(['a', 'b', 'c'])\n    'a'\n    >>> longest(['a', 'bb', 'ccc'])\n    'ccc'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not strings:\n        return None\n\n    # Initialize with the first string to correctly handle the tie-breaker rule\n    current_longest_str = strings[0]\n    max_length = len(strings[0])\n\n    for s in strings[1:]:\n        s_length = len(s)\n        if s_length > max_length:\n            current_longest_str = s\n            max_length = s_length\n            \n    return current_longest_str", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/8": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/8", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nclass SumOperation:\n    \"\"\"Computes the arithmetic sum of a list of integers.\"\"\"\n    def compute(self, numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the sum of all integers in the list.\"\"\"\n        current_sum = 0\n        for num in numbers:\n            current_sum += num\n        return current_sum\nclass ProductOperation:\n    \"\"\"Computes the arithmetic product of a list of integers.\"\"\"\n    def compute(self, numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the product of all integers in the list.\"\"\"\n        current_product = 1\n        for num in numbers:\n            current_product *= num\n        return current_product\ndef sum_product(numbers: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of integers, return a tuple consisting of a sum and a product of all the integers in a list.\n    Empty sum should be equal to 0 and empty product should be equal to 1.\n    >>> sum_product([])\n    (0, 1)\n    >>> sum_product([1, 2, 3, 4])\n    (10, 24)\n    \"\"\"\n    integer_list_instance = IntegerList(numbers)\n\n    sum_op = SumOperation()\n    product_op = ProductOperation()\n\n    calculated_sum = sum_op.compute(integer_list_instance.numbers)\n    calculated_product = product_op.compute(integer_list_instance.numbers)\n\n    result = SumProductResult(calculated_sum, calculated_product)\n    \n    return (result.sum, result.product)", "base_status": "fail", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [[[]]], "plus_fail_tests": [[[2, 4, 6, 8, 10]]]}], "HumanEval/9": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple\nclass Validator:\n    \"\"\"Handles validation of input types.\"\"\"\n    def ensure_list(self, numbers) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Raises TypeError if the input is not a list.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"rolling_max: input must be a list of integers\")\n\n    def ensure_all_ints(self, numbers: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Raises TypeError if any element in the list is not an integer.\"\"\"\n        for x in numbers:\n            if not isinstance(x, int):\n                raise TypeError(\"rolling_max: input must be a list of integers\")\nclass InputSequence:\n    \"\"\"Provides read-only access to the input list.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, values: List[int]) -> None:\n        self._values = values\n        self._length = len(values)\n\n    def is_empty(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the sequence is empty.\"\"\"\n        return self._length == 0\n\n    def get(self, index: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Retrieves an element by index.\"\"\"\n        return self._values[index]\nclass OutputSequence:\n    \"\"\"An append-only container for building the result list.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._values: List[int] = []\n\n    def append(self, value: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Appends a value to the sequence.\"\"\"\n        self._values.append(value)\n\n    @property\n    def values(self) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the accumulated list of values.\"\"\"\n        return self._values\nclass RollingMaxFunction:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates validation and computes rolling maximums.\"\"\"\n    def compute(self, numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Validates input and computes the rolling maximum sequence.\"\"\"\n        self.validate_input(numbers)\n\n        input_seq = InputSequence(numbers)\n        output_seq = OutputSequence()\n\n        if input_seq.is_empty():\n            return output_seq.values\n\n        running_max: int\n        for i in range(input_seq._length):\n            current_value = input_seq.get(i)\n            if i == 0:\n                running_max = current_value\n            else:\n                if current_value > running_max:\n                    running_max = current_value\n            output_seq.append(running_max)\n\n        return output_seq.values\n\n    def validate_input(self, numbers: List[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Delegates input validation to the Validator class.\"\"\"\n        validator = Validator()\n        validator.ensure_list(numbers)\n        validator.ensure_all_ints(numbers)\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment\n    in the sequence.\n    >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n    [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    rmf = RollingMaxFunction()\n    return rmf.compute(numbers)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/13": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "def greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" Return a greatest common divisor of two integers a and b\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n    1\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/10": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "def is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\ndef _longest_palindromic_suffix(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Returns longest palindromic suffix (used by make_palindrome) \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    for i in range(len(string)):\n        suffix = string[i:]\n        if is_palindrome(suffix):\n            return suffix\n    return \"\" # Should not be reached for non-empty strings as a single character is a palindrome.\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    if is_palindrome(string):\n        return string\n\n    # Find the longest palindromic suffix of the string.\n    longest_suffix = _longest_palindromic_suffix(string)\n\n    # The prefix to reverse is the part of the original string before this suffix.\n    prefix_to_reverse = string[:len(string) - len(longest_suffix)]\n\n    # Append the reversed prefix to the original string to form the palindrome.\n    return string + prefix_to_reverse[::-1]", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/19": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/19", "solution": "from typing import List, Dict, Set\nclass Numeral:\n    \"\"\"Domain model for a numeral token with name and numeric value.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, name: str, value: int):\n        self.name = name\n        self.value = value\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f\"Numeral(name='{self.name}', value={self.value})\"\n\n    def __lt__(self, other):\n        \"\"\"Allows direct comparison of Numeral objects based on their value.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(other, Numeral):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self.value < other.value\nclass NumeralMapping:\n    \"\"\"Provides mapping between textual numerals and integer values; defines allowed tokens constant.\"\"\"\n    NAME_TO_VALUE: Dict[str, int] = {\n        'zero': 0, 'one': 1, 'two': 2, 'three': 3, 'four': 4,\n        'five': 5, 'six': 6, 'seven': 7, 'eight': 8, 'nine': 9\n    }\n    ALLOWED_TOKENS: Set[str] = set(NAME_TO_VALUE.keys())\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def map(name: str) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Maps a numeral name to its integer value.\"\"\"\n        return NumeralMapping.NAME_TO_VALUE[name]\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_allowed(name: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a numeral name is allowed.\"\"\"\n        return name in NumeralMapping.ALLOWED_TOKENS\nclass TokenList:\n    \"\"\"Normalizes whitespace and tokenizes input into ordered textual tokens.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, raw_input: str):\n        self._tokens: List[str] = [token for token in raw_input.strip().split(' ') if token]\n\n    def tokenize(self) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the list of normalized tokens.\"\"\"\n        return self._tokens\nclass SortOperation:\n    \"\"\"Stateless sorter for List[Numeral] by numeric value ascending.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def sort(tokens: List[Numeral]) -> List[Numeral]:\n        \"\"\"Sorts a list of Numeral objects by their numeric value.\"\"\"\n        return sorted(tokens, key=lambda n: n.value)\ndef sort_numbers(numbers: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Input is a space-delimited string of numberals from 'zero' to 'nine'.\n    Valid choices are 'zero', 'one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five', 'six', 'seven', 'eight' and 'nine'.\n    Return the string with numbers sorted from smallest to largest\n    >>> sort_numbers('three one five')\n    'one three five'\n    \"\"\"\n    token_list_instance = TokenList(numbers)\n    tokens = token_list_instance.tokenize()\n\n    if not tokens:\n        return ''\n\n    numerals: List[Numeral] = []\n    for t in tokens:\n        if not NumeralMapping.is_allowed(t):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid token: {t}\")\n        value = NumeralMapping.map(t)\n        numerals.append(Numeral(t, value))\n\n    sorted_numerals = SortOperation.sort(numerals)\n\n    names = [n.name for n in sorted_numerals]\n    return ' '.join(names)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/17": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/17", "solution": "from typing import List\nclass BeatMapping:\n    \"\"\"Holds the legend mapping and resolves tokens to integers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.legend: dict[str, int] = {\n            'o': 4,\n            'o|': 2,\n            '.|': 1\n        }\n\n    def get_beats(self, token: str) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Returns the beat count for a given musical token.\"\"\"\n        return self.legend.get(token, 0)\nclass OutputList:\n    \"\"\"Holds the resulting ordered list of beat integers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, beats: List[int]):\n        self.beats = beats\n        self.length = len(beats)\nclass MusicParser:\n    \"\"\"Coordinates tokenization, mapping, and construction of the output list.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, beat_mapping: BeatMapping):\n        self._beat_mapping = beat_mapping\n\n    def parse_music(self, music_string: str) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Parses the music string, tokenizes, maps, and returns a list of beats.\"\"\"\n        tokens = self._tokenize(music_string)\n        beats_list: List[int] = []\n        for token_value in tokens:\n            beats_list.append(self._map_token(token_value))\n        \n        output_container = OutputList(beats_list)\n        return output_container.beats\n\n    def _tokenize(self, music_string: str) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"Splits the input music string into individual note tokens.\"\"\"\n        return music_string.split()\n\n    def 